\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The Euler product}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{The [finite-field partition functions](fdip-008O) attach one complex
number #{Z_p(Q)} to a symmetric integral matrix at each odd prime. For a
nondegenerate form that number is a signed Legendre symbol times an
explicit normaliser. The product of those normalised factors is an
#{L}-function of the [discriminant character](fdip-00A5).}

\p{Such a matrix is a Gram matrix of a free #{\mathbb Z}-module #{L} of rank
#{n} with a symmetric bilinear pairing valued in #{\mathbb Z}. The induced
pairing on #{\det L} takes the same signed integer value on either
generator, because the two generators differ by a sign and that sign
squares away. Every integral Gram determinant equals this integer. The
ideal it generates records the primes of degeneracy and loses the sign
used by #{\chi_D}. Its rational square class loses the prime factors of
#{2\det Q} omitted from the product. The identity uses the signed integer.}

\p{Let #{Q} be a symmetric #{n\times n} integral matrix with
#{\det Q\neq 0}. Write #{d_{\pm}(Q)} for its signed discriminant, the integer
#{(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}\det Q} named by the finite-field evaluation, and
let #{\chi_D} be the primitive character of that integer in the
discriminant note. When #{d_{\pm}(Q)} is a square, this character is
the trivial character of conductor one; write #{D=1} in that case. For
an odd prime #{p} that does not divide #{\det Q}, the signed evaluation
gives
##{Z_p(Q)=\Bigl(\frac{d_{\pm}(Q)}{p}\Bigr)g^{\varepsilon}p^{m},}
where #{n=2m+\varepsilon} and #{\varepsilon} is #{0} or #{1}. The Gauss
sum satisfies #{g^2=\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)p\neq 0}, so the quotient
#{\hat Z_p(Q)=\frac{Z_p(Q)}{g^{\varepsilon}p^{m}}}
equals #{\bigl(\frac{d_{\pm}(Q)}{p}\bigr)}. For #{\operatorname{Re}s>1},
##{\prod_{p\nmid 2\det Q}\bigl(1-\hat Z_p(Q)\,p^{-s}\bigr)^{-1}
=L(s,\chi_D)
\prod_{p\mid 2\det Q,\ p\nmid D}
\bigl(1-\chi_D(p)\,p^{-s}\bigr).}
No prime divides #{D=1}, so in the square case the second product runs
over every prime dividing #{2\det Q}. The comparison point with the
\vocabk{quadratic center character}{fdip-0049} is #{s=2}.}
\proof{
\p{The signed evaluation is the identity quoted above, and the quotient
by #{g^{\varepsilon}p^{m}} is the displayed Legendre symbol. That symbol
equals #{\pm 1}. A prime in the left-hand product does not divide
#{2\det Q}, so it is odd and does not divide #{d_{\pm}(Q)}. The
discriminant note therefore gives
#{\bigl(\frac{d_{\pm}(Q)}{p}\bigr)=\chi_D(p)}, and
#{\hat Z_p(Q)=\chi_D(p)} at every such prime.}

\p{The uncompleted product
#{L(s,\chi_D)=\prod_p\bigl(1-\chi_D(p)p^{-s}\bigr)^{-1}}
is the one fixed in the center note, absolutely convergent for
#{\operatorname{Re}s>1}. For #{\operatorname{Re}s>1} every factor
#{1-\chi_D(p)p^{-s}} is nonzero. The left-hand product is that product
with the finitely many primes dividing #{2\det Q} removed, so it equals
#{L(s,\chi_D)} divided by
#{\prod_{p\mid 2\det Q}\bigl(1-\chi_D(p)p^{-s}\bigr)^{-1}}.
Division by an inverted factor is multiplication by the factor itself.
At a prime dividing #{D}, #{\chi_D(p)=0}, so
#{1-\chi_D(p)p^{-s}=1} and that prime drops out. The quotient is
therefore #{L(s,\chi_D)} times
#{\prod_{p\mid 2\det Q,\ p\nmid D}\bigl(1-\chi_D(p)\,p^{-s}\bigr)},
which is the right-hand side. Both sides are equal as convergent
products, not as a truncation.}
}

\p{When #{n} is odd, #{\varepsilon=1}, and the normaliser retains one
factor #{g}. The sign #{\,(-1)^m} already sits inside the Legendre
symbol, so #{\hat Z_p(Q)} is that real symbol. This section does not
choose a square root of #{p}, and it does not replace #{g} by a complex
unit.}

\p{The product omits #{2} and every prime divisor of #{\det Q}. At an
odd prime where the rank of #{Q} drops to #{r}, the same finite-field
note gives #{Z_p(Q)=p^{n-r}Z_p(Q')} for a nondegenerate block #{Q'} of
rank #{r}, and #{p^n} when #{r=0}. That value is not the quotient by
the nondegenerate normaliser of rank #{n}. The prime #{2} lies outside
because the diagonalization divides by #{2}.}

\p{On the massive four-cycle, #{n=4} and #{d_{\pm}(Q)=45}. Since
#{45=9\cdot 5} and #{5\equiv 1\pmod 4}, the discriminant of that
integer is #{D=5}. The omitted primes that do not
divide #{5} are #{2} and #{3}. Under the Kronecker convention named
in the center note,
#{\chi_5(2)=\bigl(\frac{5}{2}\bigr)=(-1)^{(5^2-1)/8}=-1}. Since
#{5\equiv 2\pmod 3} and #{2^{(3-1)/2}\equiv-1\pmod 3},
#{\bigl(\frac{5}{3}\bigr)=-1}. The character note gives
#{\chi_5(3)=\bigl(\frac{5}{3}\bigr)}. The prime #{5} divides #{D},
so #{\chi_5(5)=0} and that factor is not restored. The identity
specialises to
##{L(s,\chi_5)\,(1+2^{-s})\,(1+3^{-s}).}
At #{s=2} the two restored factors are #{5/4} and #{10/9}.}

\p{The correspondence with formal libraries is partial. Mathlib's
\leanref{legendreSym.quadratic_reciprocity} is the law that exchanges
the two arguments of a Legendre symbol. This section does not use that
law. Mathlib's \leanref{DirichletCharacter.LFunction} agrees, for real
part greater than #{1}, with the Dirichlet series
#{\sum_n\chi(n)n^{-s}}. Neither declaration identifies
#{\hat Z_p(Q)} with #{\chi_D(p)} or assembles the product above.}

\p{The identity does not evaluate #{L(s,\chi_D)} in closed form. In
particular it does not derive #{4\pi^2/(25\sqrt 5)}.}

\p{A function of #{L(s,\chi_D)} does not recover a trace ratio. That
obstruction is the [fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042), which concerns
invariants of the quadratic form alone. The product above does not
evaluate a Weil index, and it is not identified with the real
normalizer.}
